course: everyday
lesson: 203
title: Jokes and teasing: when is it still funny?
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A good joke is one everybody can laugh at, including the person it is about. Saying «just joking» after something mean does not make it okay; if the target is hurt, it was not a joke to them.
explain: Some untrue words are jokes, not lies. Sarcasm, like «Great weather!» in pouring rain, means the opposite of the words, shown by the tone, and the speaker expects you to understand.
explain: Friendly teasing, like a nickname you both enjoy, can be a sign of closeness. Teasing that hurts and goes on, about looks, bodies, accents or what someone likes, is bullying.
explain: Some jokes are never okay: about someone's race, religion or disability, which spread prejudice, or about bombs and weapons, which staff at airports and schools must treat seriously.
explain: Example: a boy in Belfast keeps calling a classmate «Four Eyes» because of his glasses. The classmate says calmly «Stop, I don't like that» and walks away, and tells a teacher when it goes on. Being teased about how you look is never your fault.
fact: A joke is only a joke if everyone, including the target, can laugh.
fact: «Just joking» does not make a hurtful remark okay; you can say «That wasn't funny to me».
fact: Jokes that mock people's race, religion or disability cause real hurt and spread prejudice.
fact: Sarcasm means the opposite of the words and is shown by tone; it is a joke, not a lie.
fact: Inside jokes can make others feel left out, so include them or explain.
fact: If a friend slips and gets hurt, first check that they are okay.
fact: Never joke about bombs, weapons or threats; it can cause alarm, searches and serious trouble.
fact: Friendly nicknames are fine when both people enjoy them.
fact: Teasing that hurts and goes on is bullying.
fact: If you are teased about your looks, say «Stop, I don't like that», walk away, and tell an adult if it continues.
fact: Speaking more than one language is a skill, and mocking accents is unkind.
fact: A «joke» that makes someone worried or late is not fun for them.
fact: Teasing about weight or bodies can hurt for years and can lead to eating problems.
fact: Not every joke lands, and that is okay; humour depends on the moment and the people.
exercise: You tell a joke and nobody laughs. What is a good reaction? | Laugh it off and try another time | Explain it again until they laugh | Get cross with the people listening | Never tell a joke again
exercise: Someone says something mean, then adds «just joking». What is true? | It is still not okay, and you can say «That wasn't funny to me» | It is fine, because he said it was a joke | You must laugh along, or everyone will say you have no sense of humour | It is only mean if he says it twice
exercise: A classmate makes a joke mocking someone's religion. What should you do? | Not laugh, and say it is not okay or tell a teacher | Laugh quietly so you are not left out | Make an even funnier joke about it | Repeat it later to your other friends to see if they laugh at it too
exercise: You and a friend share an inside joke in front of others. What is kind? | Include the others or explain the joke | Keep laughing so they feel curious | Tell them it is a secret they can't know | Whisper it so they cannot hear
exercise: Someone says «Great weather!» in pouring rain. What is this? | Sarcasm, meaning the opposite, shown by tone | A small lie to make you put your coat on and go outside | A mistake about the weather | A true remark about the rain
exercise: A friend slips and hurts herself. What should you do first? | Check if she is okay | Laugh, because falling looks funny | Film it to show others later | Walk away so she is not embarrassed
exercise: Your little brother tells you a silly knock-knock joke. What is a good reaction? | Play along and laugh, even if it is silly | Tell him it is not funny at all | Tell him the real ending of the joke that grown-ups use | Ignore him until he stops
exercise: Why must you never joke about bombs at an airport? | Staff must take it seriously, causing alarm and trouble | Airport staff do not understand jokes | Jokes are not allowed anywhere in public | Only adults with a ticket are allowed to make jokes there, not children
exercise: Your friend calls you a funny nickname you both like. Is that okay? | Yes, friendly nicknames are fine when both enjoy them | No, all nicknames are bullying | Only if a teacher agrees to it | Only if you have known each other for years and years since nursery
exercise: Someone keeps calling a classmate a name he hates. What is right? | Stop if it is you; otherwise speak up or tell an adult | Join in so the name does not bother you | Leave it, since names cannot hurt | Wait until he cries before doing anything, then you know it hurts
exercise: You are teased about your braces. What can you do? | Say «Stop, I don't like that», walk away, and tell an adult if it goes on | Tease them back about their looks | Hide your smile so nobody sees your braces | Believe it is your own fault for having braces and try to smile without teeth
exercise: Someone mocks a classmate's accent. What should you do? | Stand up for her or stay with her | Copy the accent to make others laugh | Tell her to speak like everybody else | Pretend you did not hear anything
exercise: Someone hides a classmate's bag «as a joke» and she gets upset and is late. What should he do? | Give it back and say sorry | Keep hiding it, since it is only a joke | Tell her to stop being so upset | Hide it again tomorrow somewhere easier
exercise: Why is teasing about someone's weight so harmful? | It can hurt for years and lead to eating problems | Because weight is not a funny subject to most people | It is only harmful when adults do it | It is not harmful if it is meant kindly
exercise: An older brother teases you all the time. What helps? | Tell him how it makes you feel, and ask a parent for help if it continues | Tease him back even harder | Never speak to him again | Hide in your room until he grows up and moves out, and then it will all stop
test: A girl in Sacramento says «Oh, brilliant!» when her ice cream falls on the ground. Is she lying? | No, it is sarcasm, and everyone understands she means the opposite | Yes, because she says it is brilliant | Yes, because she wants to trick her friends into thinking she is happy | No, she really thinks it is brilliant
test: Two boys tease each other with silly nicknames and both laugh. One day one boy goes quiet and looks down. What should the other do? | Stop and ask if he is okay with it | Keep going, since they always do it | Use a stronger nickname to cheer him up | Tell others the nickname too
test: A boy jokes at school that he has a gun in his bag. Why is this serious even as a joke? | Staff must treat it as real, causing alarm and serious trouble | Because someone might laugh too loudly | It is serious only if the teacher hears it, otherwise it is just a laugh | Because school bags are not allowed at all
test: A girl's classmates laugh at her because she speaks Spanish at home. What is true? | Speaking two languages is a skill, and mocking it is unkind | She should stop speaking Spanish at home so that she fits in better | It is fine because they are only joking | It is her fault for mentioning it
test: How can you tell friendly teasing from bullying? | Bullying hurts the person and keeps going | Bullying always involves hitting | Friendly teasing is always about looks, bullying is always about grades | There is no difference between them
test: A friend trips over in the playground and grazes her knee badly. Some children laugh. What should you do? | Check if she is hurt and help her | Laugh too, so you fit in with the others | Pretend not to know her | Tell her it looked funny
test: A boy says a joke about wheelchair users and adds «it's just a joke». What is the problem? | Mocking a disability hurts people and spreads prejudice | The joke was simply not very well told | It is fine if no wheelchair user hears | The problem is only if a teacher or a wheelchair user is nearby
test: Your friends keep making an inside joke while a new girl sits with you. What is kind? | Explain the joke or stop, so she is included | Keep going so she wants to be part of your group and learns the joke | Tell her she will understand it one day | Ask her to sit somewhere else
test: A girl with freckles is called «Spotty» every day. Whose fault is the teasing? | The teasers', never hers | Hers, for having freckles | Nobody's, as it is only words | Hers, if she does not laugh along
test: he called me a name then said jk, so its fine? | No, you can say that wasn't funny to me | Yes, jk makes it okay | Yes, just laugh along | Yes, if he laughed too it was just a joke between you
test: my mate slipped on the ice lol it was so funny | First check that she's okay | Film it for later so you can show everyone at school | Laugh louder | Walk away
test: my mate goes "oh great" when it starts raining, is he lying? | No, that's sarcasm, a joke shown by tone | Yes, he says the opposite of what he means, so it's a lie | Yes, he loves rain | He's confused
test: thought it'd be funny to say theres a bomb in my bag at the airport | Never joke about bombs, it causes alarm and serious trouble | Fine if you laugh after | Fine if it's obviously a joke and you laugh straight away after | Fine if you're a kid
explain: A boy in Leeds was called a nickname about his weight every day as a "joke"; after months he stopped eating lunch and would not go to school, and only then did the teachers find out.
explain: A girl in Sydney laughed along when friends teased her accent, but at home she cried and asked her mum to move schools; her friends had no idea, because she had smiled.
fact: Teasing that goes on and on can hurt a child as much as hitting, and can lead to sadness that lasts for years.
fact: A smile or a laugh does not prove the other person thinks the joke is funny; many children laugh just to hide that they are hurt.
test: we always call him "tubby", he laughs so it's fine right | No, people often laugh to hide being hurt, stop it | Yes, if he laughs it's only a joke and nobody is hurt | Yes, nicknames are normal | Only if a teacher hears it
test: A girl who is teased every day starts to say she feels sick each morning. What is likely happening? | The teasing is making her afraid to go to school | She has just caught a different cold every single morning | She just wants a day off | Teasing never affects how people feel
test: kids keep mocking my voice, they say its banter | Tell them to stop and tell an adult if it goes on | Put up with it, banter is normal and everyone gets some | Mock them back louder | Stop talking at school
test: did u hear a kid in year 5 joked about having a bomb in his bag and the whole school got evacuated, police came and everything. real lesson? | Never joke about bombs or weapons; it causes alarm | Bomb jokes are fine if your friends laugh | Only say it quietly so teachers don't hear | Schools overreact to anything said at all
test: everyone's laughing at that new girl's accent, my mate said she cried in the toilets. lol. pass it on? | No, mocking accents is unkind and so is spreading it | Yes, it's just a funny story | Yes, it's only teasing, not bullying | Yes, if you don't say her name
test: my cousin reckons a boy got teased about his weight so bad he never ate lunch again for like 3 years. what's probably blown up and what's true? | The exact story may be stretched, but such teasing really hurts for years | It's all made up; teasing never matters | It's all true, every word, because a cousin said it | Teasing about weight only hurts adults
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a remark that wounds its target but is described as «just joking»? | The description does not render the remark acceptable | The description renders any remark acceptable | It is acceptable if the speaker is a friend | It is acceptable if others present laugh
exam: Identify the principal reason for which jokes concerning bombs or weapons are prohibited in public places. | They may cause alarm, searches and serious consequences | They are seldom considered amusing by adults | They are permissible only on aeroplanes | They are prohibited solely in schools
exam: It is given that a pupil laughs at a joke directed at himself. Select the most accurate inference. | The laughter does not prove that he finds the joke amusing | The laughter proves that he is not hurt | The laughter obliges the joke to be repeated | The laughter shows that he began the joke
exam: Ascertain the condition under which teasing is properly characterised as bullying. | When it causes hurt and is persistently repeated | When it occurs on a single occasion only | When it is undertaken by a friend | When it concerns hobbies and interests
